-- More Reading practice tests, one set per part: three published PRACTICE_TEST packages (IELTS_SPACE_PRO, C10_C20,
-- ACTUAL_TESTS), each with Passage 1, 2 and 3 of eight questions, so every Passage tab and Full đề has more tests to choose
-- from. The question formats follow the usual IELTS pattern for each part:
--   Passage 1: True/False/Not Given, gap filling, matching information
--   Passage 2: matching headings, multiple choice, sentence completion
--   Passage 3: Yes/No/Not Given, multiple choice, summary completion
-- Passages, questions and the step-by-step explanations are original text written for this project and still need a
-- review by an IELTS examiner. Every quoted sentence in an explanation is copied from its passage, so the review screen
-- can highlight it. Cover images are small inline SVG data URIs.

INSERT INTO content_assets (id, asset_type, text_content, media_reference, validation_status) VALUES
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000001', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000001', 'PASSAGE', 'A. Vertical farms grow salad leaves and herbs in stacked trays inside warehouses, with no soil and no sunlight. Plants sit in shallow water that carries dissolved nutrients, and rows of coloured lamps replace the sun.

B. The method uses far less water than a field. Because the water circulates in a closed system, growers say they use about a tenth of the amount needed outdoors. The leaves are also grown away from insects, so few pesticides are required.

C. Energy is the main cost. The lamps run day and night, and the largest farms spend more on electricity than on wages. Several companies are therefore building their warehouses next to wind farms in the hope of cheaper power.

D. Supporters see a future in which cities grow their own greens, cutting the distance food travels. Critics reply that the crops are mostly lettuce and herbs, which have low value, and that staple foods such as wheat could not be grown in this way.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000002', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000002', 'PASSAGE', 'A. On the island of La Gomera, shepherds once crossed deep ravines by whistling instead of shouting. The whistled speech, known as Silbo, can carry across several kilometres of broken ground, far beyond the reach of the human voice.

B. Silbo is not a code but a real language. Whistlers replace each spoken word of Spanish with a pattern of pitch and rhythm, so any sentence can be sent. Linguists were surprised to find that listeners use the same parts of the brain as when they hear ordinary speech.

C. By the 1990s the skill was fading, as roads and mobile phones removed the need for it. Fewer than a few dozen elderly people could whistle fluently, and many young islanders had never heard it.

D. The island''s government then made Silbo a compulsory subject in primary schools. Children now learn it for a few hours each week, and the language is performed at festivals. Teachers say that although few pupils will ever need it to send a message, it gives them pride in their heritage.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000003', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000003', 'PASSAGE', 'A. For centuries, people have admired the flashing of fireflies on summer evenings, yet the display is not decoration. Each species flashes in its own pattern, and a male uses it to advertise himself to females hidden in the grass. A female answers with a single flash after a precise delay, which is how he knows that she belongs to his species.

B. The system is open to abuse. In one genus, females have learned to copy the reply of another species. When an unsuspecting male lands beside her, she eats him. Biologists believe this gives her the chemicals that protect fireflies from spiders, so the behaviour is more than simple hunger.

C. Artificial light threatens the whole arrangement. Street lamps and illuminated signs are far brighter than a firefly, and males that cannot see the female''s reply may give up searching. Researchers in several countries have recorded fewer fireflies near brightly lit roads. I find the evidence persuasive, although most of the studies are small.

D. Simple changes could help. Switching off lights after midnight, using warmer colours and shielding lamps so that they point downward all reduce the glare. Some towns have already adopted such rules, and the writer hopes more will follow before the displays vanish from the places where they were once most common.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000004', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000004', 'PASSAGE', 'A. Audiobooks were once sold as boxes of cassettes for people who could not read print, such as the blind. Today they are among the fastest-growing parts of publishing, and one in five adults in several countries listens to at least one a year.

B. Technology explains much of the change. Smartphones let listeners carry hundreds of titles in a pocket, and downloads removed the need for bulky boxes. Commuters and joggers, who cannot easily read, have become a large new audience.

C. The performance matters as much as the text. Publishers now hire trained actors, and some recordings use a full cast with music and sound effects. A few authors read their own books, though editors admit that a good writer is not always a good narrator.

D. Not everyone is convinced. Some readers argue that listening is a lesser form of reading because the mind wanders more easily. Researchers who tested the idea found that comprehension was similar for both, provided the material was not too difficult.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000005', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000005', 'PASSAGE', 'A. More than seventy per cent of the Earth is covered by sea, yet far less of the seabed has been mapped in detail than the surface of the Moon. Until recently, scientists relied on ships towing single instruments along narrow lines, which took years to cover even a small area.

B. Sound changed the work. A ship sends pulses of sound to the bottom and times the echo, and from that delay computes the depth. Modern systems fire hundreds of beams at once, so a single voyage can chart a strip of seabed several kilometres wide.

C. Maps have practical uses. Cable companies need to avoid steep slopes where underwater landslides can snap a line, and fishing fleets use the charts to find the ridges where fish gather. Geologists also hope to learn where earthquakes begin.

D. The task remains huge. A global project aims to complete the picture by the end of the decade, using not only research ships but also unmanned boats and the voyage records that merchant vessels already collect. Even then, the project''s leaders admit, the finest details will be missing.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000006', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000006', 'PASSAGE', 'A. For most of the twentieth century, farmers in the highlands of Ethiopia and Eritrea sold little teff, a tiny grain used for flatbread, because yields were low and the stalks fell over in the rain. Wheat and maize, promoted by aid programmes, replaced it in many fields.

B. Attitudes have changed. Teff contains no gluten, which makes it attractive to people who cannot eat wheat, and it is rich in iron and fibre. Restaurants in Europe and North America now serve it, and the price paid to growers has risen sharply.

C. Plant breeders have begun to improve it. By crossing varieties with stronger stalks, they have produced plants that stand up in heavy rain and give a larger harvest. In my view the most exciting result is that these new strains still taste the same, since earlier attempts at improvement often spoiled the flavour.

D. Challenges remain. Teff seed is so small that sowing by machine is difficult, and most of the work is still done by hand. Some farmers fear that rising demand abroad will push prices beyond the reach of local families, who eat teff every day. It would be a pity if a crop saved by its own people were then lost to them.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000007', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000007', 'PASSAGE', 'A. In many villages without a mains supply, a single solar panel on a roof now powers a few lamps and a phone charger. Households that once paid heavily for kerosene find the panels repay their cost in under two years.

B. The change reaches beyond lighting. Children can study after dark, and shopkeepers keep drinks cold in small refrigerators. Health workers store vaccines at the correct temperature, which was impossible when the nearest cold room was a day''s journey away.

C. Many families pay for panels in small instalments sent by mobile phone. If a payment is missed, the panel switches itself off until the next one arrives, which lets companies lend to customers with no bank account.

D. Problems remain. Cheap batteries wear out after two or three years, and few villages have anyone able to mend a broken panel. Several charities now train local technicians, believing that repairs matter as much as sales.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000008', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000008', 'PASSAGE', 'A. Cities are often several degrees warmer than the countryside around them. Dark roofs and roads soak up sunshine during the day and release it slowly at night, so the heat lingers long after sunset. Engineers call this effect the urban heat island.

B. One response is to plant the roofs. A layer of soil and plants over a flat roof absorbs less heat than bare tar, and water evaporating from the leaves cools the air above. Measurements on hot afternoons show roof surfaces up to thirty degrees cooler.

C. The benefits go further than comfort. Buildings with green roofs need less energy for air conditioning, and the plants hold back rainwater that would otherwise overload the drains in a storm. Some roofs also provide a habitat for insects and birds.

D. The drawbacks are real. A green roof is heavy, so many older buildings cannot carry it without costly strengthening, and the plants need watering during dry spells. Some cities now give grants to owners, hoping that the cost will fall as the method spreads.', NULL, 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-010000000009', 'IMAGE', NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml;base64,', 'VALID'),
    ('2f000000-0000-4000-8000-020000000009', 'PASSAGE', 'A. Few experiences are more irritating than standing in a queue, yet the length of the wait is less important than people assume. Psychologists have found that time seems to pass more slowly when we are idle, so a short wait with nothing to do can feel longer than a long one that is filled with activity.

B. Fairness matters even more. A single line feeding several counters is judged fairer than separate lines, because no one sees another customer served first. Companies that switched to a single line recorded fewer complaints, although the average wait did not change. It is surprising that so simple a change is still rare.

C. Some businesses go further by managing expectations. A theme park posts a waiting time above the entrance to each ride, and deliberately overstates it. Visitors who reach the front sooner than promised leave pleased, whatever the real length of the wait.

D. Critics say these tricks treat customers as fools. I would disagree: the methods reduce frustration without cheating anyone of anything. A better test, in my view, is whether a business also works to shorten the queue itself, since no amount of psychology replaces a faster service.', NULL, 'VALID')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

